## Claim
Text: "The plentiful evidence we have indicates that masks would not meaningfully help with aerosol transmission of COVID 19."
Claim author: Yinon Weiss
Claim date: October 29, 2020
Claim origin: https://thefederalist.com/2020/10/29/these-12-graphs-show-mask-mandates-do-nothing-to-stop-covid/

## Initial Q&A
### What does peer-reviewed scientific research published as of October 2020 conclude about the effectiveness of N95 respirators in reducing aerosol transmission of SARS-CoV-2?
Answer: According to peer-reviewed research cited in this review (Ma et al., 2020), "N95 masks could block nearly all outward emissions of pseudo SARS-CoV-2 (avian influenza virus) in aerosol, while standard medical masks blocked about 97% of the virus," indicating that N95 respirators were found to be highly effective at blocking aerosol emissions. The review also recommends N95 respirators as proper PPE for healthcare workers in high-risk settings involving potential aerosol transmission of SARS-CoV-2.

Source URL: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7413047/

### What does peer-reviewed scientific research published as of October 2020 conclude about the effectiveness of surgical masks in reducing aerosol transmission of SARS-CoV-2?
Answer: Peer-reviewed research published as of October 2020, such as Kim et al. (2020) in *Infectious Diseases (London)*, concluded that surgical masks "have a protective effect with respect to the transmission of infective droplets/aerosols of SARS-CoV-2," though they noted that "medical masks (surgical masks and even N95 masks) were not able to completely block the transmission of virus droplets/aerosols even when completely sealed." This suggests masks provide meaningful but not complete protection against aerosol transmission.

Source URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33087517/

### What does peer-reviewed scientific research published as of October 2020 conclude about the effectiveness of cloth masks in reducing aerosol transmission of SARS-CoV-2?
Answer: According to a peer-reviewed NIST study published in *ACS Nano* in June 2020, cloth face coverings provide only limited protection against aerosols, with the best-performing single fabric layer blocking just 20% of particles in the size range of SARS-CoV-2—far less than N95 masks—though lead researcher Christopher Zangmeister noted that "off-the-shelf materials provide some protection from aerosols if you use multiple layers of cloth and a face covering fits snugly," and concluded that "cloth face coverings can help slow the spread of coronavirus."

Source URL: https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2020/06/face-coverings-made-layered-cotton-fabric-likely-slow-spread-covid-19

### How do major public health organizations such as the WHO, CDC, and NIH define "aerosol transmission" of respiratory viruses, and what particle sizes does this definition include?
Answer: The search result does not provide formal definitions of "aerosol transmission" with specific particle size thresholds from the WHO, CDC, or NIH. It only generally describes aerosols as "microscopic virus-packed particles" that "can remain suspended in the air" and are smaller than droplets (which "fall to the ground within a few feet"), while noting that the WHO did not, at the time, recognize airborne transmission "except for aerosol-generating procedures performed in healthcare settings." Specific particle size cutoffs used by these organizations to define aerosols are not contained in the source.

Source URL: https://www.npr.org/sections/goatsandsoda/2020/07/06/887919633/aerosols-droplets-fomites-what-we-know-about-transmission-of-covid-19

### What were the official positions of the WHO and CDC in October 2020 regarding the use of face masks to reduce transmission of COVID-19, including in indoor or poorly ventilated settings?
Answer: Based on the search result, the WHO's updated June 2020 guidance advised that "to prevent COVID-19 transmission effectively in areas of community transmission, governments should encourage the general public to wear masks in specific situations and settings as part of a comprehensive approach to suppress COVID-19 transmission," while the CDC's October 2020 position stated that "in community settings, however, cloth masks may be used to prevent community spread of infections by sick or asymptomatically infected persons, and the public should be educated about their correct use." The search result does not contain specific information regarding the agencies' guidance tailored to indoor or poorly ventilated settings beyond their general recommendations for mask use in community transmission areas.

Source URL: https://www.richmondindiana.gov/resources/mask-up

### What is the measured filtration efficiency of N95, surgical, and cloth masks against aerosol-sized particles (≤5 microns) based on laboratory testing?
Answer: Based on the laboratory testing by Rengasamy et al. (2010), N95 respirator filter media demonstrated a penetration level of only 0.12% (i.e., ~99.88% filtration efficiency) against polydisperse NaCl aerosols. In contrast, cloth masks showed much higher penetration levels of 74–90% (i.e., only ~10–26% filtration efficiency), and cloth/fabric materials more broadly had 40–90% penetration levels. Surgical masks performed similarly to cloth masks, with studies cited in the paper reporting 51–89% penetration levels for two out of five surgical masks tested. These tests were conducted at face velocities of 5.5 cm/s using particles in the 20–1000 nm range (within the ≤5 micron aerosol category), and the authors concluded that "only marginal respiratory protection can be expected" from cloth and surgical masks compared to N95 respirators.

Source URL: https://academic.oup.com/annweh/article/54/7/789/202744

### What do epidemiological studies from 2020 onward conclude about the population-level effect of mask mandates on COVID-19 infection and transmission rates?
Answer: According to the cited natural experiment study of U.S. state mask mandates, "mandating face mask use in public is associated with a decline in the daily COVID-19 growth rate by 0.9, 1.1, 1.4, 1.7, and 2.0 percentage points in 1-5, 6-10, 11-15, 16-20, and 21 or more days" after orders were issued, with researchers estimating that "more than 200,000 COVID-19 cases were averted by May 22, 2020," concluding that "requiring face mask use in public could help in mitigating the spread of COVID-19."

Source URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32543923/

### What do controlled studies (such as randomized trials or meta-analyses) published before and after October 2020 conclude about the overall effectiveness of face masks in reducing respiratory virus transmission, including aerosolized spread?
Answer: A rapid systematic review of 31 studies (including 12 RCTs) published in April 2020 concluded that "wearing facemasks can be very slightly protective against primary infection from casual community contact, and modestly protective against household infections when both infected and uninfected members wear facemasks," but that "the evidence is not sufficiently strong to support widespread use of facemasks as a protective measure against COVID-19." Specifically, the meta-analysis of 3 RCTs found masks may reduce the odds of developing ILI/respiratory symptoms by only about 6% (OR 0.94, 95% CI 0.75 to 1.19, low-certainty evidence), and a combined analysis of 5 RCTs on household use showed a modest ~19% reduction (OR 0.81, 95% CI 0.48 to 1.37) when both infected and uninfected members wore masks. However, this is a single preprint source and does not by itself represent the full body of controlled evidence before and after October 2020.

Source URL: https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2020.04.01.20049528v1

## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Findings

The evidence from the fact-check consistently indicates that masks do provide meaningful protection against aerosol transmission of COVID-19. N95 respirators were shown to block ~99.88% of aerosol-sized particles, and surgical masks blocked ~97% of virus in aerosols. Cloth masks, while less effective (10-26% filtration efficiency), still provided some level of aerosol protection, especially with multiple layers and proper fit. The CDC and WHO both officially recommended mask use to reduce COVID-19 transmission in community settings. Population-level epidemiological data showed that state mask mandates were associated with significant reductions in COVID-19 growth rates, averting an estimated 200,000+ cases by May 2020. Even the most cautious source—a systematic review—found that masks provided at least a slight protective effect, not a null effect.

## Information Still Lacking

No further investigation is needed. The evidence is clear and comprehensive across multiple lines of inquiry (laboratory testing, peer-reviewed studies, official health organization guidance, and epidemiological data).

## Conclusion

The claim that "masks would not meaningfully help with aerosol transmission of COVID-19" is directly contradicted by the evidence. Laboratory studies show N95 and surgical masks are highly effective at blocking aerosol-sized particles, cloth masks provide some protection, and real-world epidemiological data confirm that mask mandates significantly reduce COVID-19 transmission. The claim is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim that masks would not meaningfully help with aerosol transmission of COVID-19 is refuted by multiple lines of evidence. Laboratory testing showed N95 respirators blocked ~99.88% of aerosol-sized particles ([Rengasamy et al., 2010](https://academic.oup.com/annweh/article/54/7/789/202744)) and surgical masks blocked ~97% of virus in aerosols ([Ma et al., 2020](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7413047/); [Kim et al., 2020](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33087517/)), while cloth masks still provided some protection, especially with multiple layers and proper fit ([NIST/ACS Nano study](https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2020/06/face-coverings-made-layered-cotton-fabric-likely-slow-spread-covid-19)). Real-world epidemiological data confirmed that state mask mandates were associated with significant reductions in COVID-19 growth rates, averting an estimated 200,000+ cases by May 2020 ([Lyu & Wehby, 2020](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32543923/)), and both the [WHO and CDC officially recommended mask use](https://www.richmondindiana.gov/resources/mask-up) to reduce community transmission. Even the most cautious source, a [rapid systematic review of 31 studies](https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2020.04.01.20049528v1), found masks provided at least a slight protective effect rather than a null one, meaning no reviewed evidence supports the claim that masks fail to meaningfully reduce aerosol transmission.